 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "THE FAERIE GLEN. Every hill here is a perfect green cone, like a giant pressed a thumb into the ground. It's real, a place on Skye with the same strange shape. Nobody knows who built it. The sheep do not say."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "A RING OF STONES. Please stay on the path, speak softly and don't take anything home with you except photos. A fairy hound watches over the glen. It has a great deal of patience and a great deal of teeth."
  },
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "faerie_glen_landslip",
   "title": "Made by a Landslip",
   "text": "The real Faerie Glen sits above Uig on Skye, and its cones, terraces and little lochans were left by ancient landslips, the same slumping ground that made the Quiraing and the Old Man of Storr. The crag locals call Castle Ewen is only old lava. Please do not tell the fairies."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "faerie_glen_sithean",
   "title": "The Sìthean",
   "text": "In Gaelic a sìthean (SHEE-an) is a fairy hill, and the daoine sìth, the fairy folk, were said to live inside. Old Highland advice: never eat their food, never take a gift without thanks, and always call them the Good People. Flattery works on fairies, as on most people."
  }
 ],
